1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,504
How do you know the person signing up really owns that email address?

2
00:00:03,554 --> 00:00:07,128
Anyone can type anyone's email into a sign-up form.

3
00:00:07,178 --> 00:00:12,432
So the bank does what a postcard with a code does: it sends a one-time code to that address.

4
00:00:12,482 --> 00:00:15,264
Only someone who can read that inbox can type it back.

5
00:00:15,314 --> 00:00:17,208
That is email verification.

6
00:00:17,258 --> 00:00:20,184
The code works for a short time and for one sign-up.

7
00:00:20,234 --> 00:00:26,832
Too many wrong codes, and the sign-up is locked until the window ends, even if a new code is asked for.

8
00:00:26,882 --> 00:00:32,688
The password never touches ecom's own database: it goes straight to the login server.

9
00:00:32,738 --> 00:00:37,512
And if the store that keeps the codes is down, nothing is sent and nothing is accepted.

10
00:00:37,562 --> 00:00:41,976
The right code creates the merchant, and makes the person who signed up its first admin.

11
00:00:42,026 --> 00:00:45,456
Next: how a merchant earns the right to take money.
